A||N||12||vandya||Burning brakes on right rear||||9||
Z||000000||vandya||07-31-2001||08:35 AM||||Does anybody have any idea of what might cause the right rear brakes to smoke.  I've changed both rear calipers, rotors, and brakes.  The car does not stop well.  It looks like the rear brakes are the only ones working.  I recently bought the car from someone else and they said that they placed a master cylinder on the car.  I think this may be the problem.  Does anybody have any ideas of what the problem may be.  ||||reg||9
Z||000001||isthiswhereiputausername?||07-31-2001||09:02 AM||||possible the emergency brake is sticking..<BR>also possible the rubber brake hose is and causing the fluid to stick the caliper..<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Patrick86GT||07-31-2001||10:53 AM||||Assuming pre-88 brakes and you don't have a fluid leak onto the rotor, and that you did not adjust the screw out caliper piston too tight to the rotor, then check the right side e-brake cable for freedom of travel. Might be a quick check to disconnect it at the caliper and see if the problem disappears.  <P>Good luck||||reg||1
Z||000003||vandya||07-31-2001||06:00 PM||||Thanks for the info.  The emergency brake cable was removed from the car, but I will check the the rubber brake hose.  I forgot to mention that the left rear caliper works fine, but the right rear is the only caliper smoking.   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by isthiswhereiputausername?:<BR><B>possible the emergency brake is sticking..<BR>also possible the rubber brake hose is and causing the fluid to stick the caliper..</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||hodaka||07-31-2001||06:48 PM||||i have the same problem with my right front brake.  there is new rotors and pads but it still smells like its burning.||||reg||1
Z||000005||artherd||07-31-2001||07:38 PM||||Replace all 4 of your brake hoses.  <P>The rubber can collapse INSIDE the hoses, and clog your lines!! (flush and drain all calipers too.)<P>PS: you need to use the ebrake to ajust the rear calipers, please put it back on!<P> <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000006||SilverFieroV6||07-31-2001||10:05 PM||||IT is prob one of two thing  either the caliper is locked up and rubbing on the rotor  or the line has collapsed.  It happened to my car last week.   <BR>I would suggest fixing it right away.  Here is the detials on what happened. <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/008968.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/008968.html</A> <BR>Sam<P><P>------------------<BR>Gold 86 SE V6 4sp daily driver<BR>Silver 85 SE V6 auto ( actually running now)||||reg||1
Z||000007||qwikgta||07-31-2001||11:16 PM||||HOSE...HOSE...HOSE... This happened to me on both my 87 and 88.  It was only after I changed the caliper that I found out it was the dame CHEAP hose the whole time<P><BR>ROb||||reg||1
Z||000008||sparks||08-01-2001||09:14 AM||||Ok so the e-brake is needed for rear caliper adjustment???  How's that?  I thought disk brakes were self adjusting.<P>Anyway, I'm asking because I have the same problem.  I've adjusted my brake cable all the way out and there's still not enough slack in it to let it fully release on the left side.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Any suggestions?  I know it's the e-brake dragging.<P>No, not hoses, they're stainless and brand new.<P>Calipers are rebuilt units from Advance Auto (Parts America).  <P>Rotors are new too.<P>I disconnected the ebrake on the left side and had no more problems with it dragging this morning. <P>So what's worse, drive it without the lever on the back of the caliper or put the lever on the caliper and hope nothing hits it and sets the parking brake?  <P>Until I can figure out why my cable doesn't have enough slack in it?????  I have to drive it without it hooked up.<P>------------------<P>Adam Sparks<p>[This message has been edited by sparks (edited 08-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Ken Wittlief||08-01-2001||10:31 AM||||whatever the cause is - dont drive the car like that until you get it fixed.<P>  I got to put out my THIRD car fire about a month ago - some guy in an old ford had a dragging front brake, and it caught fire. I came up on the scene and saw the flames under his front wheel, and the kid is standing about 100 yards away from his car.<P>  I ran over to see what was buring - the brake hose had melted and was dripping brake fluid down by the front wheel, the puddle was burning, his tire was starting to burn, and the grease on his ball joints and tierod ends was burning.<P>  I had my wifes car - both my cars have fire extinquishers mounted in the trunk (cause I have put two other cars out in the past - neither were mine BTW) but hers doesnt have one.<P>  i opened all his doors and hood to ventelate the car - the fire department had already been called, and I found a gallon of washer fluid in the back seat. I managed to get the flames out with that about a minute before the sherriffs car pulled up with a CO2 extinquisher.<P>   the volunteer fire department arrived about 9 minutes later.<P>  the whole point is, dragging brakes CAN get hot enought to set your car on fire.||||reg||11
Z||000010||sparks||08-01-2001||11:32 AM||||Right now it's not dragging, it's not hooked up at all.  So no problems with heat, I drove it to work this AM after disco'ing the e-brake.  However I would like to know if anyone has any suggestions about what could be wrong with it.  Cable between the handle and the adjuster not moving as much as it should????  I've sprayed down the portion that's not covered with rubber with penetrating oil to loosen it up if it were seized.<P>It was HOT after my wife turned around and came back home, she barely made it 1.5 miles and figured out something was wrong and brought it back to me and took the VW to work instead.  <P>After I drove it to work this AM it's not hot since I disconnected the ebrake cable.<P>------------------<BR><BR>Adam Sparks||||reg||4
Z||000011||vandya||08-04-2001||10:01 PM||||Thanks for the info, it turned out to be the brake hoses.  I replaced them and the smoke disappeared.  <P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by isthiswhereiputausername?:<BR><B>possible the emergency brake is sticking..<BR>also possible the rubber brake hose is and causing the fluid to stick the caliper..</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000012||EdsB52||08-05-2001||12:52 PM||||I was going to suggest air in the front brake lines, but it's already fixed <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">. The front brakes obviously have priority as far as the master cylinder is concerned, so if there was air in the front front brake lines, there would actually be more fluid pressure going to the rear.||||reg||1
